 ## 4. Users, Roles & Permissions
 
 Every authenticated user holds exactly one PRIMARY role plus any number of additional ones, and every one of them is internal JobsAdmire staff — the app has no external-user role at all since `investor` retired with the Investments module on 2026-09-14 (§8 "Removed domains"). There is no partner scoping, no employer/sourcing portal, no data-isolation firewall.
 
 **Authorization is `key × action × scope`, database-driven, resolved in ONE place.** A key is a module (`sales`) or a module plus one screen (`hrm.payroll`); a grant is a row on a role, or a row on a USER (an override); `EffectivePermissionsService` gathers the rows and `resolveEffective()` — a pure function in `@jobsadmire/permissions`, run by the backend AND the browser — turns them into answers. Every route says which it is (`@RequirePermission`, `@SelfService()` or `@Public()`) or the container does not start; every admin page has a line in the route manifest or nobody can open it; and **no code decides access by comparing a role slug** — the handful of slug comparisons that remain are declared policies about WHOSE account may be touched, not about what anybody may do, and a repo invariant refuses a new one.
 
 > **The permission programme, W0–W4 (2026-09-15).** Everything above was built in five waves over one day, and the sections below describe the RESULT rather than the sequence. W0 fixed the security bugs the audit found (the grant boundary enforced in the roles API, versioned role defaults so the seed stopped undoing operators' edits, `Role.isActive` made load-bearing, all 13 actions in the catalog). W1 built the engine: the key grammar, per-user overrides, the one resolver, boot-time conformance. W2 put that resolver in the BROWSER, typed every key, and gave Marketing the first screen-level keys, the route manifest and the page guard. W3 took screen keys to all 22 modules, drove the route census to zero and the unmapped-page list to zero, and made the page guard fail closed. W4 — this state — turned strict mode on, replaced the last hard-coded authorization with grants, declared the rules that are not permissions, purged the phantom grant rows, and wrote this down. Incident-level detail for any single wave lives in the Decisions & gotchas below, dated.
 
 ### 4.1 Permission model (Key × Action × Scope)
 
