 **Decisions & gotchas**
 
 - **Don't grant the entire `roles` module — or `users:CREATE` / `users:DELETE` — to any role except `super-admin`.** Admin holds `users:VIEW + EDIT` so they can edit existing accounts and reset/change passwords; that's the only relaxation. Granting `users:CREATE` to a non-super lets them mint a new super-admin account; `users:DELETE` lets them remove one; handing out `roles` lets a role redefine its own grants.
 - **The activity timeline is permission-gated per type (closed 2026-08-19).** `GET /activities/timeline` and `/activities/by-subject` were **auth-only**, on the documented reasoning that "the caller had to know the subject/context id to ask in the first place". Ids are guessable and seeded ones are literals, so any authenticated user — an internal recruiter, or the external **investor** role of the day — could read `contextType=investments&contextId=div-2025` and get back `declared the dividend "FY2025 Dividend" (USD 750,000)` (verified live before the fix; that module was removed 2026-09-14, §8 "Removed domains", and its nine mappings went with it). An activity `summary` is a rendered sentence *about* the entity, so reading a timeline is reading the entity. `ACTIVITY_TYPE_SUBJECTS` in `activities.controller.ts` maps every `subject:`/`context:` type to its owning CASL subject + action and the controller enforces it — **fail closed**, an unmapped type 403s, so a new activity type must add its mapping in the same change set (`ENDPOINT_BY_KIND` precedent). The removed investor-relations types were the precedent for mapping an action **stricter than `read`** when a module's VIEW grant is deliberately narrower than its timeline: pick the action that the timeline's *content* really requires, not reflexively `read`.
 - **`isInternal()` is DELETED — never reintroduce it (2026-08-19 audit).** It returned `true` unconditionally (every Operations user is internal staff) but was written in the shape of a permission check, so **nine** callsites read `if (!isInternal(user)) throw new ForbiddenException('Only Super Admin can manage users')` and every one was dead code. The consequence was a live privilege-escalation path: `role-admin` holds `users:EDIT`, and `GET /users` hands out every account's `roleId` + role slug, so an admin could `PATCH /users/<self>` with the super-admin `roleId`, or `POST /users/<owner>/change-password` (no current-password proof, and it revokes the victim's sessions), and re-login as a super-admin — which short-circuits to `can('manage','all')` and unlocks everything, including `roles:*`, which is granted to **no role at all** (`copilot:*` was the other such module until the Copilot module was removed 2026-09-14, §8 "Removed domains"). `UsersService.assertMayTargetUser()` now refuses any operation aimed at a super-admin unless the caller is one (a TARGET check — an admin resetting a recruiter's password is the point of `users:EDIT`), `update()` additionally refuses a `roleId` resolving to super-admin and refuses **any** self-role change, and `disableTwoFactor`'s force path was gated on `isSuperAdmin` (it had been reachable by every authenticated user, recruiters included — latent only because nobody had 2FA on yet). **W4 split that last one in two**, because the two halves are different kinds of rule: the CAPABILITY is `users:EDIT` — the grant that already resets a password, which is the other half of taking an account — and the TARGET rule stays an identity check, since no cell can express "…but not the owner's account". `admin` therefore gained force-disable on non-super-admin targets, which is consistent with the password reset it already had. **A guard that cannot fail is worse than no guard, because it reads like one in review.**
 - **Never write `include: { <a User or HrmPerson relation>: true }` (2026-08-19 audit).** A bare Prisma `include` on a relation returns EVERY column of that model. Eight detail (`findOne`) reads in `hrm/lifecycle.service.ts` did this while their list siblings all used explicit `select`s — so `GET /hrm/documents/:id` returned the uploader's `passwordHash`, `twoFactorSecret` and backup codes, plus the employee's national ID, passport, tax ID, bank account and IBAN, behind nothing more than `hrm:VIEW`. Confirmed live: every production document was uploaded by a super-admin, so a super-admin's bcrypt hash was in the response. Use the `SAFE_USER_SELECT` / `SAFE_PERSON_SELECT` constants at the top of that file. A repo-wide sweep found no other offender — keep it that way.
 - **Multi-role (2026-08-20): a user has ONE primary role plus any number of ADDITIONAL roles.** `User.roleId` is unchanged and still means "the role" — it rides in the JWT as `roleSlug`, drives the super-admin bypass, and is what every pre-existing read means. `user_additional_roles` layers extra roles on top, and effective permissions are the **union**; where two roles grant the same `(module, action)` at different scopes the **widest** wins (`ScopeFilterService` already reduced over `SCOPE_STRENGTH`, so widening its query was enough). Deliberately additive: a database with no rows in that table behaves exactly as before.
   **`UserRolesService.roleIdsFor()` is the single resolver, and FOUR readers must all go through it** — `CaslAbilityFactory.createForUser` (the actual gate), `PermissionsService.getMyPermissions` (drives `<Can>` and the module nav), `PermissionsService.effectivePermissions` (the user-detail matrix), and `ScopeFilterService` (row-level scope). Miss one and the halves disagree: the UI hides what the backend allows, or worse the reverse. A fifth reader of `user.roleId` goes through it too.
   **`super-admin` may never be an additional role.** The bypass reads the JWT's *primary* `roleSlug`, so it would grant nothing while looking like it granted everything — and would become a silent escalation route past the `roles:*` / `users:CREATE` boundary if the bypass were ever widened. `UserRolesService` refuses it, `assertMayTargetUser` still guards the target, and `PUT /users/:id/additional-roles` is `users:EDIT`-gated. Caching: per-role permission sets stay keyed by `roleId` (so changing a role still invalidates for everyone holding it); the per-user additional-role list is a separate `user-roles:<userId>` key, busted on write.
   **Changing the PRIMARY role reconciles the additional roles, and `setAdditionalRoles` DROPS the primary role rather than refusing the write (fixed 2026-08-26).** The invariant is "a user's additional roles never include their primary role". `UserRolesService.setAdditionalRoles()` enforced it by throwing `400 That is already this user's primary role`, but nothing enforced it on the *other* write path: `PATCH /users/:id` could move a user INTO a role they already held as an extra and left the row behind. That is precisely what happened to Sana Nasir (`marketing-specialist` granted as an extra on 2026-08-20, made her primary role on 2026-08-26), and it bricked the account — the user-detail page seeds its checkbox state from `GET /users/:id/additional-roles` while **hiding the primary role from the rendered checkboxes**, so the stale id rode along in every payload with no way to untick it and every "Save additional roles" 400'd forever. The operator saw only "it will not save". Two changes, both needed: (1) `UsersService.update()` deletes any `user_additional_roles` row for the incoming primary role **in the same transaction as the role change**, then busts `user-roles:<userId>`; (2) `setAdditionalRoles()` filters the primary role out of the payload instead of throwing — correct SET semantics (the endpoint takes the roles held *in addition to* the primary one, so the primary is by definition not among them), and it makes the endpoint self-healing for rows that predate the fix. The refusals for **super-admin, inactive and unknown** roles stay hard errors: those are requests that cannot be honoured, not requests that are already satisfied. **General lesson: enforcing an invariant with a hard rejection only works if every path that can break it is closed — otherwise the guard stops punishing the writer who broke it and starts punishing whoever touches the record next.**
 
   **The additional-roles payload is anchored on the PERSISTED primary role, never on the edit form's dropdown (2026-08-26, caught in review of the fix above).** The first cut of the fix derived the payload as `extraRoleIds.filter(id => id !== editData.roleId)` — and `editData.roleId` is the unsaved Primary-role `<select>` draft, while "Save additional roles" is a *separate* button that posts immediately. Because `PUT /users/:id/additional-roles` is a **SET** (`deleteMany` with `notIn: wanted`), any id the client omits is DELETED. So merely changing the dropdown — without saving the profile — would strip that role from the payload and the server would silently delete a real grant, then show a success toast; the server's own filter cannot compensate, because it compares against the primary role it still has. It now anchors on `userData.data.roleId` (the persisted value) and the checkbox list filters by the same value, so the payload and the screen cannot disagree. The seeding `useEffect` was also deleted: state now holds only the *pending edit* (`extraRolesDraft`, `null` = "show the server's answer"), which is what stops a refetch from clobbering unsaved ticks. **General lesson: when an endpoint replaces a set, never derive its payload from unsaved form state — an omission is a deletion, and a half-edited form becomes a silent revocation.**
 - **Anything that asks "does this user hold role X?" must read the PRIMARY role AND the active ADDITIONAL roles (2026-08-31).** `UserRolesService.roleIdsFor()` returns primary + additional and is what CASL, `PermissionsService` and `ScopeFilterService` all resolve through — its own doc comment warns that a fifth reader of `user.roleId` has to go through it too. `AssigneeEligibilityService` was that fifth reader and did not: it matched `roleId: { in: eligibleRoleIds }`, i.e. the primary column only. Nisanur Karaman was given `internal-recruiter` as an **additional** role on a `sales-manager` primary (2026-08-26), so she genuinely held `internal-recruitment:EDIT` and could open and work every IR screen — and was still absent from **Assign to recruiter**, with nothing on screen to explain it. Both methods now match primary-or-active-additional (`holdsAnyOf()`), and `assertEligible()` checks the same union, so the picker and the write path cannot disagree. **General lesson: a permission model with two sources of role membership grows a silent half-state the moment one reader consults only the first — the user can DO the work but cannot be GIVEN it, and neither screen shows an error.** The same omission was then swept out of every recipient resolver on 2026-08-31: `NotificationsService.notifyModuleViewers()` (21 call sites), `EventDispatcher.getApprovers()` and `getOfferLetterOwnerSigners()`, and `ProjectRecipientsService.approverIds()` all now use the shared `holdsModuleGrant(module, action)` fragment from `common/utils/role-helpers.ts` (with `holdsAnyRole(roleIds)` beside it for the id-list case). **`EventDispatcher.getSuperAdmins()` was deliberately LEFT primary-only**, as is every `userRole: { slug: 'super-admin' }` arm inside the shared fragment: `setAdditionalRoles()` refuses super-admin, so an additional-role arm there could only ever match zero rows while reading, to the next person, as licence to widen the bypass itself. `offer-letters:SIGN_AS_OWNER` was weighed separately rather than waved through for consistency — prod holds **zero** `offer-letters` grant rows of any action, so widening delegates nothing and cannot resurrect the 2026-08-05 `admin` revocation; what it fixes is the reverse failure, since all six SIGN_AS_OWNER routes already union additional roles via CASL and a narrow resolver would let someone open the signature queue and sign while never being told there was anything to sign. The staff pickers used to carry an `investor` role EXCLUSION (`task.service.ts`, `campaign.service.ts`, `project-members.service.ts`), documented here as correct at primary-only and never to be unioned — negating a union flips "holds X" into "holds none of them", which removes staff from a picker rather than admitting anyone. The filters went with the role on 2026-09-14 (§8 "Removed domains"); **the rule stands for any future negative role filter.** Measured blast radius on the day: **zero** users changed notification status, because no additional role in prod currently grants `hrm:VIEW`, `settings:VIEW`, `internal-recruitment:APPROVE`, `projects:APPROVE` or `offer-letters:SIGN_AS_OWNER` — this is a latent-correctness fix that closes the hole before it bites. Shape is pinned by `common/utils/role-helpers.spec.ts`, including an explicit test that the super-admin arm stays primary-only.
 - **The roles/users grant boundary is now enforced in the DATABASE, not just the seed matrix (2026-08-19 audit).** The rule — no role except super-admin may hold `roles:*`, `users:CREATE` or `users:DELETE`, because either lets a role mint or remove a super-admin — was honoured by `seed.ts` but never enforced against roles created through the **Roles UI**. Prod had a violator: a CUSTOM role `field-agent-legacy-cmqf5e`, displayed as *"Field Agent (legacy cmqf5e) — executes assigned project & physical-meeting tasks (no approve/delete)"*, actually carried `roles:{V,C,E,D}` + `users:{V,C,E,D}` + `settings` full + `hrm`/`investments`/`internal-recruitment` full at scope ALL — **12 admin-tier grants, more than `role-admin` itself has (8)**. Its only holder was an inactive QA account, so it was not live-exploitable, but the *label* was the real hazard: anyone assigning it from the Roles UI would read "field agent, no approve/delete" and be handing out super-admin minting rights. Migration `20260819160000_enforce_role_user_grant_boundary` deletes every `roles` grant and every `users` CREATE/DELETE grant unconditionally (super-admin needs no rows — it bypasses CASL), and `20260819170000_narrow_legacy_field_agent_role` then reset that role to mirror the seeded `role-field-agent` (owner decision: `projects` V/C/E @ ASSIGNED_TO_ME + `communications` V/C/E + `notifications` VIEW — 73 grants deleted, 7 inserted), so its existing description is finally accurate and it is safe to assign. **When auditing permissions, read `role_permissions` in the database, not just `ROLE_PERMISSION_MATRIX` — runtime-created roles never appear in the seed, and this one carried more admin-tier grants than `role-admin` while being labelled a field agent.**
-- **Built-in role DEFAULTS are versioned; the Roles UI is the truth (2026-09-15, owner decision D1 — W0 of the permission programme).** `scripts/deploy-vps.sh` step [6] runs `prisma db seed` with `FORCE_SEED_PROD=yes` on **every** deploy, and the seed's per-row upsert forced `allowed: true` in its UPDATE branch — so every grant an operator unticked in `/admin/roles` came back within two minutes of the next push, silently, and the same loop rewrote each built-in role's `description`. The seed no longer decides: `Role.defaultsVersion` (migration `20260922100000_role_defaults_version`, SYSTEM roles stamped to 1 because prod already holds the version-1 matrix) records how much of `prisma/role-defaults.ts` a role has been given, and the seed applies only entries whose `since` exceeds it, **create-if-missing — the upsert's UPDATE branch is deliberately empty**, then stamps `ROLE_DEFAULTS_VERSION`. `description` left the role upsert's `update` for the same reason. Editing rules live in that file's header: **adding** a grant needs `since: ROLE_DEFAULTS_VERSION + 1` plus a bump of the constant; **removing** one still needs a paired migration (the seed only ever creates — the `internal-recruiter` projects revocation, `20260819141000`, is the precedent); **changing** an existing entry's scope or action list changes nothing on an existing install, so edit it in the UI or write a migration. `SEED_RESET_ROLE_DEFAULTS=true` wipes and re-creates each matrix role's grants for deterministic e2e fixtures — mapped in `docker-compose.yml` only, and a spec asserts it is absent from `docker-compose.vps.yml` (which passes env by an explicit `environment:` list). `defaultsVersion` stays 0 on the three CUSTOM roles and means nothing there. **A one-file invariant spec** (`test/permissions/role-defaults.unit.spec.ts`) pins version == max(`since`), no duplicate cell per role, no boundary cell, and no unknown action string — the four mistakes that would otherwise only surface on a production deploy.
+- **Built-in role DEFAULTS are versioned; the Roles UI is the truth (2026-09-15, owner decision D1 — W0 of the permission programme).** `scripts/deploy-vps.sh` step [6] runs `prisma db seed` with `FORCE_SEED_PROD=yes` on **every** deploy, and the seed's per-row upsert forced `allowed: true` in its UPDATE branch — so every grant an operator unticked in `/admin/roles` came back within two minutes of the next push, silently, and the same loop rewrote each built-in role's `description`. The seed no longer decides: `Role.defaultsVersion` (migration `20260922100000_role_defaults_version`, SYSTEM roles stamped to 1 because prod already holds the version-1 matrix) records how much of `prisma/role-defaults.ts` a role has been given, and the seed applies only entries whose `since` exceeds it, **create-if-missing — the upsert's UPDATE branch is deliberately empty**, then stamps `ROLE_DEFAULTS_VERSION`. `description` left the role upsert's `update` for the same reason. Editing rules live in that file's header: **adding** a grant needs `since: ROLE_DEFAULTS_VERSION + 1` plus a bump of the constant; **removing** one still needs a paired migration (the seed only ever creates — the `internal-recruiter` projects revocation, `20260819141000`, is the precedent); **changing** an existing entry's scope or action list changes nothing on an existing install, so edit it in the UI or write a migration. `SEED_RESET_ROLE_DEFAULTS=true` wipes and re-creates each matrix role's grants for deterministic e2e fixtures — mapped in `docker-compose.yml` only, and a spec asserts it is absent from `docker-compose.vps.yml` (which passes env by an explicit `environment:` list). `defaultsVersion` stays 0 on the three CUSTOM roles and means nothing there. **A one-file invariant spec** (`test/permissions/role-defaults.unit.spec.ts`) pins version == max(`since`), no duplicate cell per role, no boundary cell, and no unknown action string — the four mistakes that would otherwise only surface on a production deploy. **Version 3 (WP3a, 2026-09-19)** added the website module's rows — `admin` `website` V/C/E/D/EXPORT, `marketing-manager` six CMS resource rows, `sales-manager`/`sales-executive`/`sales-agent` `website.forms` VIEW — the first resource-key rows the matrix carries (`website-inheritance.unit.spec.ts`).
 - **Built-in (SYSTEM) roles cannot be renamed or deactivated (2026-09-15).** `RolesService.update` refused only the `super-admin` slug, so the other eleven were editable through the Roles UI — futilely, since the seed resets `name`/`slug`/`type`/`isActive` by fixed id on every deploy, but expensively: a rename could trip the seed's squatter-rename block into relabelling whichever OTHER row held the canonical name, and a deactivation now genuinely revokes every holder's permissions (see the deactivation gotcha below) and is then silently reverted — an access outage that heals itself and leaves nothing to look at. `description` stays editable in both halves (it is what the Roles pencil is for, and the seed no longer overwrites it). The detail page disables the name field for a SYSTEM role and omits `name` from the payload.
 - **A deactivated role now actually revokes permission (2026-09-15).** `Role.isActive` shipped with the Roles UI, is displayed in the roles list, and is refused by `setAdditionalRoles` — but **nothing on the enforcement path read it**. `UserRolesService.roleIdsFor()` filtered inactive ADDITIONAL roles; the PRIMARY role was never checked anywhere, so deactivating a role did nothing at all for the people who actually held it while looking in the UI like a revocation. It is decided once, in `CaslAbilityFactory.getPermissionsForRole` — an inactive (or missing) role yields no rules, and the empty answer is cached like any other — and every other read of a user's grants carries `role: { isActive: true }` **on the query, not on the id list**, so the primary and additional arms are covered by one clause: `PermissionsService.getMyPermissions` / `getEffectivePermissions`, `ScopeFilterService.resolveScope` / `assertAnyGrant` / `strongestScope`, `ProjectAccessService.hasWorkBypass`, and `holdsModuleGrant`'s PRIMARY arm (the additional arm always had it; the asymmetry kept a deactivated role's holders in every recipient resolver — notified about work the guard would then refuse them). The super-admin arm stays primary-only. **Both caches are busted when `isActive` flips**: `permissions:{roleId}` and each additional-role holder's `user-roles:{userId}` key (`UserRolesService.invalidateHoldersOf`) — without the second the revocation lands up to five minutes late, long enough for the operator to conclude it did not work.
 - **`POST /users` refuses a super-admin or inactive destination role (2026-09-15).** `update()` has carried the DESTINATION guard since the 2026-08-19 audit; `create()` never had it, so the shorter version of the same escalation — mint the account directly in the super-admin role, then log in as it — was open to any holder of `users:CREATE`. Only a super-admin holds that grant today, which is exactly why the guard matters: the boundary has to survive the grant being handed out by mistake. Both doors also refuse an inactive role now, since moving a user into one produces a login that sees the shell and nothing else with no error to explain it.
 - **`internal-recruiter` holds NO `projects` grant (revoked 2026-08-19, owner decision).** It had been seeded `projects` V/C/E @ ALL, which put the whole Projects & Tasks module on a recruiter's icon rail and gave them write access to the shared company/contact directory — while nothing in the recruitment workflow reads or writes projects. Revoked in the seed matrix **plus** migration `20260819141000_revoke_recruiter_projects` (the per-row-upsert seed never deletes out-of-matrix grants). If a recruiter ever needs field work, give them the `field-agent` role as well rather than widening this one.
 - **Historical (superseded 2026-09-14 — the Reports module itself was removed, §8 "Removed domains"): `reports:VIEW` is permission to open the Reports surface, NOT permission to read every domain's numbers (tightened 2026-08-19).** `/reports/*` was one controller serving several modules, and gating it on `reports:VIEW` alone meant `internal-recruiter`, `marketing-manager`, `sales-manager` and `training-coordinator` — none of whom hold `hrm:VIEW` — could read the company's monthly **payroll totals**. Endpoints returning another module's data now require that module's VIEW **as well**, via the new `@RequirePermissions(...)` (AND semantics; `CaslAbilityGuard` normalises single-object and array metadata, +6 tests): `payroll-trends` and `employee-retention` need `hrm:VIEW`; `project-performance`, `agent-performance`, `meeting-statistics`, `expense-analytics`, `physical-meeting-completion` and `campaign-roi` need `projects:VIEW`. `overview` (counts only) and the sales/training/recruitment reports stay on `reports:VIEW`. Gating on the owning module *instead of* `reports:VIEW` would have gone the other way and exposed spend data to `field-agent`/`sales-agent`, so both grants are required — don't "simplify" it back to one.
 - **Recruitment-platforms is admin-config-only; `internal-recruiter` holds VIEW + `REVEAL_CREDENTIALS` only (2026-05-30).** A recruiter reads the shared LinkedIn/Indeed passwords to log in (fully audited) but cannot CREATE/EDIT/DELETE platform accounts — a single mis-edit of the shared account silently breaks every recruiter's posting flow. The narrowing was the seed matrix **plus** migration `20260530130000_narrow_recruiter_platform_perms` (the per-row-upsert seed never deletes out-of-matrix grants).
